e of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c67f98e447853b9489432463be401ef6e7cedb7246a49161b503e29f4c4fc198"} {"question" "q-162224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A cube"] ["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A parallelogram"] ["text" "A point"] ["text" "A triangle"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A point"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/957d55e3fb66bb2633798cf13d5c6049ffa4524ca585515ffaf88771ee32fe9b.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is a zero matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a point on the plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c67f98e447853b9489432463be401ef6e7cedb7246a49161b503e29f4c4fc198"} {"question" "q-162222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A line segment"] ["text" "A parallelogram"] ["text" "A point"] ["text" "A square"] ["text" "A triangle"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "q-162222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A point"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/state.json" "file_sha256" "1ecba6ddb51ebffbbf3aecdfa2dffaedfca50f5520271906b7096c58abde30e6"} {"question" "e-12428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- The transformation is invertible" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12428.json" "file_sha256" "00632c566fc807694cded4ddb23055c29b050b5466e7b0560edb777cd48bee07"} {"question" "e-12428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-2 \\\\ v-u+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0,$ the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12428.json" "file_sha256" "00632c566fc807694cded4ddb23055c29b050b5466e7b0560edb777cd48bee07"} {"question" "e-12429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix form of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12429.json" "file_sha256" "f6126aafe3c44761e31fb90eeedaebf81f686c7df5024ce0cf1f7abdc55d66e7"} {"question" "e-12429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 7-(-9)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12429.json" "file_sha256" "f6126aafe3c44761e31fb90eeedaebf81f686c7df5024ce0cf1f7abdc55d66e7"} {"question" "e-12430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 2, \\qquad y = 4u - 5v + 9.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ of $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}x - y + 1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12430.json" "file_sha256" "055315708f08e35378f3efd65c9df61b5be1e940226273bde5cc39bf158bf6bc"} {"question" "e-12430" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+2 \\\\ 4u-5v+9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ 4u-5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-5)-(-1)\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y-9) \\\\ 4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y-9)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5x-y-1 \\\\ 4x-y+1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{5}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{(-1)}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{4}x - y + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 5 + (-1) + 4 = 8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12430.json" "file_sha256" "055315708f08e35378f3efd65c9df61b5be1e940226273bde5cc39bf158bf6bc"} {"question" "e-12441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/assets/8797a50ddc0750a97190f96d8bc1cffe70c6ef9cc8e55a46f5a75d6b6702c81d.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12441.json" "file_sha256" "9d657ab79fc63d80565029a6fd65b5d2ba652afafbce44e87f9ed965dc3ff004"} {"question" "e-12441" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040441/example-12441.json" "file_sha256" "9d657ab79fc63d80565029a6fd65b5d2ba652afafbce44e87f9ed965dc3ff004"}] "usage" {"e-12441" [] "q-124249" [] "e-12428" [] "e-12430" [] "q-124111" [] "q-162151" [] "e-12429" [] "q-124317" []} "basis" 1445 "retractions" [[17592186179924 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = u - v, y = u + 2v + 1$.\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $det(A) = 0$\n3. The transformation is invertible"] [17592186179936 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind $a + b + c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186179937 :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$x = 5v - 7u + 3, y = 7v - 10u + 5$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n$u = ax - 5y + 4, v = bx + cy + 5$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?"] [17592186180020 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3707/Source/Images/q-162151.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T$?"] [17592186179360 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$x = u - v - 2, \\qquad y = v - u + 5$.\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix representing the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\det(A) = 0$\n3. The transformation is invertible"] [17592186179360 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The given transformation can be expressed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v - 2 \\\\ v - u + 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ -u + v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)$.\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\det(A) = 0$, the affine transformation is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186179361 :question/problem "The matrix form of the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind $a+b+c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186179361 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAu + b &= x \\\\\nAu &= x - b \\\\\nu &= A^{-1}(x - b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $\\mathbf T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/((-4) \\cdot 7 - (-9) \\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A^{-1}) \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(x) - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = -7 + 3 + 8 = 4\n$$"] [17592186179362 :question/problem "The affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$x = u - v + 2, \\qquad y = 4u - 5v + 9$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ of $\\mathbf T$ is\n$u = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}x - y + 1$,\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?"] [17592186179362 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $\\mathbf{T}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAu + b &= x \\\\\nAu &= x - b \\\\\nu &= A^{-1}(x - b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v + 2 \\\\ 4u - 5v + 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ 4u - 5v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(1 \\cdot (-5) - (-1) \\cdot 4)\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A^{-1}) \\cdot (\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(x) - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x - 2 \\\\ y - 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\cdot (x - 2) + (-1) \\cdot (y - 9) \\\\ 4 \\cdot (x - 2) + (-1) \\cdot (y - 9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5x - y - 1 \\\\ 4x - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{5}x - y + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{(-1)}, \\qquad v = \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{4}x - y + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 5 + (-1) + 4 = 8\n$$"] [17592186179365 :question/problem "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nDescribe the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3707/Source/Images/4252f221506b6465a4575a94777c7b53.png)"] [17592186179365 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation\n$\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbf{u} \\longrightarrow A \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{b}$,\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., when $\\det(A) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(T) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix.\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-invertible and the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177545 17592186177546 17592186177547 17592186177548 17592186177549 17592186177617 17592186177618 17592186177619 17592186177620 17592186177621 17592186177623 17592186177624 17592186177625 17592186177626 17592186177627 17592186178121 17592186178122 17592186178123 17592186178124 17592186178125] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177544 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177544 :answer-field/id] [17592186177544 :answer-field/key] [17592186177544 :answer-field/type] [17592186177616 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177616 :answer-field/id] [17592186177616 :answer-field/key] [17592186177616 :answer-field/type] [17592186177622 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177622 :answer-field/id] [17592186177622 :answer-field/key] [17592186177622 :answer-field/type] [17592186178120 :answer-field/correct] [17592186178120 :answer-field/id] [17592186178120 :answer-field/key] [17592186178120 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
